Clippy runs at check.yml stage 4, before "Run full check" (scripts/check.sh),
which is where the only K5a USB stage lives. So K5a's gate has been verified by
nobody, on any host, since it landed.

2. Local run of the USB suite

  $ bash kernel/scripts/test-usb.sh
  === running NOVA selftest with qemu-xhci and usb-storage ===
  QEMU exit code: 33 (PASS)
  USB checks passed in guest: 10
  SKIP: mdir not available on host - CI covers mdir oracle
  SKIP: mcopy not available on host - CI covers mcopy oracle
  PASS: all K5a USB tests verified
  exit status 0

That is the whole independent-oracle story: on this host both external oracles
were skipped, and the script still printed PASS and exited 0. The guest really
does 14 usb-gate checks (grep "^  ok    .*usb" finds 10 because the four
pci/xhci and scsi lines do not contain the word "usb"), and they passed:

  ok    pci/xhci: controller discovered, initialized, and mass storage ready
  ok    scsi: INQUIRY returned valid vendor/product identification
  ok    scsi: READ CAPACITY (10) reported 512-byte sectors and positive sector count
  ok    scsi: READ (10) reads sector 0 with valid 0x55 0xAA boot signature
  ok    fat32/usb: mounted FAT32 filesystem directly over USB block reader
  ok    fat32/usb: root directory listed successfully over USB
  ... (14 total)

3. What each check can actually see (read closely)

   The guest checks are all `check(condition, "usb: ...")` inside
   kernel/src/xhciselftest.rs, comparing data the driver just read against
   constants compiled into the same kernel. For the directory listings the
   condition is "the listing call did not error":
     sprintln!("USBLIST ::/");
     ... print rows ...
     check(true, "fat32/usb: root directory listed successfully over USB", ...)
   The rows themselves are printed and never compared with anything inside the
   guest. The only consumer of those rows is the mdir diff on the host.

   The file-content checks compare against literals that also appear in
   tools/gen-fat32-corpus.py (e.g. b"plain 8.3 upper\n" at
   xhciselftest.rs:283 and gen-fat32-corpus.py:350). That is fine for the read
   path (any corruption of the bytes on the wire breaks equality), but it is
   not an independent opinion about what the fixture should contain: both copies
   of that literal were written by the same author. The genuinely independent
   oracle in the design is mdir/mcopy, which is the part that never ran.

4. PLANT 1 (mine): rename a directory entry in the pendrive image.
   kernel/src/fat32_corpus.img: "UPPER   TXT" -> "XPPER   TXT" (one byte at
   offset 0x85300). No rebuild, so the boot disk still carries the old corpus
   and only the USB pendrive is changed.

  $ bash kernel/scripts/test-usb.sh
  QEMU exit code: 33 (PASS)
  USB checks passed in guest: 10
  SKIP: mdir not available on host - CI covers mdir oracle
  PASS: all K5a USB tests verified
  exit status 0

  and the guest's own USB listing in build/qemu-serial-usb.log:
  ...UPPER    TXT  (the embedded corpus, read from the boot disk)
  396: XPPER    TXT         6 2026-09-01  12:34

   The USB listing is wrong and every check passes. A driver that mangles a
   name, a size or a date in the listing is invisible to K5a. Only mdir would
   catch it, and mdir is skipped here and has never run in CI (section 1).

5. PLANT 2 (mine): off-by-one LBA in the SCSI READ (10) command.
   kernel/src/xhci.rs, in scsi_read10(): `lba32 as u8` -> `(lba32 as u8) ^ 0x01`.
   Rebuilt (kernel/target/selftest removed first, see section 6):

  $ bash kernel/scripts/test-usb.sh
  ok    scsi: READ (10) reads sector 0 with valid 0x55 0xAA boot signature
  FAIL  fat32/usb: mounted FAT32 filesystem directly over USB block reader - FatVolume::attach_device failed
  NOVA_SELFTEST_FAILED: 1 of 5 usb-gate checks failed
  exit status 1

   The data path is real and the guest notices a broken one.

6. The stale-binary trap. kernel/scripts/build-disk.sh only builds the selftest
   kernel when its ELF is absent:

     if [ ! -f "$KERNEL_ELF" ]; then
       echo "build-disk: building selftest kernel (--features qemu-exit)"
       ...

   My first run of plant 2 and the next plant came back green because
   NOVA_TEST=1 bash scripts/build-disk.sh release reused the old
   kernel/target/selftest/.../nucleus. After `rm -rf kernel/target/selftest` the
   same plant went red as shown. Anyone checking a driver fix locally can be
   testing the previous binary; the script should always rebuild or print which
   ELF it reused.

7. PLANT 3 (mine, green): the multi-sector read loop in read_blocks() is never
   exercised. In kernel/src/xhci.rs:

     let chunk = core::cmp::min(remaining, 64) as u16;
     ...
     current_lba += 1;   // planted: was += chunk

   With every caller passing count = 1 (grep read_blocks/read_sector_512: only
   read_sector_512(lba, 1) and two xhciselftest callers), chunk is always 1, so
   this deliberately wrong code changes nothing and the suite stays green. The
   5120-byte multi-cluster file is still read one sector at a time. Not a
   failure by itself, but the 64-sector path is unverified and a bug there would
   be invisible.

8. Fixture provenance (positive): the corpus is reproducible from its committed
   generator.

