9. Cited paths and counts.
   kernel/src/xhci.rs, kernel/src/xhciselftest.rs, kernel/scripts/test-usb.sh,
   docs/logs/k5-usb-selftest.log (457 lines) and docs/logs/k5-plants.log all
   exist. The numbers in the author's log check out against mine where they
   overlap: QEMU exit 33, NOVA_SELFTEST_OK, and the USBLIST rows match the
   committed corpus for the pristine image. The script's own threshold is
   `USB_OK_COUNT -lt 6` against 14 usb-gate checks, so 8 could fail and this
   particular grep would still pass; the guest's exit code is the real gate.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDINGS

F1 (blocking). No check independent of the driver has ever run for K5a. The only
   external oracles are mdir and mcopy, and on the machine where this was
   developed they are absent, which makes test-usb.sh print "SKIP ... CI covers
   mdir oracle" and then "PASS: all K5a USB tests verified" with exit 0
   (section 2). CI has never run the stage either: clippy failed at eb0e65b and
   has failed on every push since (section 1). The claim "verified against
   mdir/mcopy" is, today, unverified.

F2 (high). The USB directory listing is printed, never graded. With one entry
   renamed in the pendrive image the guest listed XPPER.TXT and every check
   passed (section 4). The listing is compared only by the mdir oracle, which
   F1 says never ran. A kernel that mis-decodes LFN, short names, sizes, dates or
   entry order over USB is currently indistinguishable from a correct one.

F3 (medium). A missing QEMU is a pass: test-usb.sh prints "SKIP: qemu-system-x86_64
   not installed - USB tests skipped" and exits 0. D4's script treats a missing
   QEMU as a harness error (exit 2); K5a's does not, so a CI image without QEMU
   would report success.

F4 (medium). build-disk.sh silently reuses a stale selftest kernel (section 6).
   I hit it: two plants appeared green against the previous binary and only went
   red after deleting kernel/target/selftest. This is the mechanism by which a
   local "plant caught" run can be false.

F5 (low). The `USB_OK_COUNT -lt 6` check is looser than the 14 usb-gate checks
   that exist, and it counts by grep rather than by what the guest ran. It is
   redundant with the exit code, but as written it would accept a run where 8 usb
   checks failed.

F6 (low). The multi-chunk read path in read_blocks() is unexercised; a bug there
   is invisible (section 7).

VERDICT: FAIL
  The driver and the FAT-over-USB path are real -- a one-bit LBA error makes the
  guest fail loudly (section 5) -- but the only checks that are independent of
  the driver (mdir/mcopy) have never run, locally or in CI, and the script calls
  the suite PASS when it skips them. A wrong USB directory listing passes
  everything (section 4). Until the mdir/mcopy oracle runs somewhere and is
  proven to catch a wrong listing, K5a's "verified against mdir" claim is
  unsupported.
